WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.008
In the year 228, Zhuge Liang led his army north to campaign against the Central Plains.

00:00:04.358 --> 00:00:10.550
Before they set out, Zhuge Liang urged him again and again: be sure to camp by the mountain, and never underestimate the enemy.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:18.388
Young Ma Su volunteered to guard Jieting, but disobeyed orders and pitched his camp on top of the mountain.

00:00:18.738 --> 00:00:22.578
He stood on the summit, looking proudly into the distance, full of confidence.

00:00:22.928 --> 00:00:26.576
But the Wei army cut off the water supply, and the Shu soldiers were thirsty and exhausted.

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:31.846
There was no water on the mountain; the soldiers' lips cracked, and morale sank lower and lower.

00:00:32.196 --> 00:00:38.196
The line was finally broken and the Shu army fell into chaos. Ma Su fled down the mountain with his battered remnant, in utter disarray.

00:00:38.546 --> 00:00:46.994
Defeated, Ma Su was escorted back to camp. With tears flowing, Zhuge Liang himself took up the brush and signed the military order, to uphold military law.

00:00:47.344 --> 00:00:56.104
The sun rose in the east; the Shu camp was orderly and its banners bright. With military law strictly upheld, the army's heart did not waver.

00:00:56.454 --> 00:01:00.726
This story reminds us that even the clever must heed the counsel of experience.

